## The Signaling Server

This is the only piece you write, and you have already written most of it: it is the chat-room broadcast from Module 2 with one change.

```{walkthrough}
title: The signaling server
step: The relay
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/server.js
region: relay
@"WebSocket.Server({ server })": Attached to the same HTTP server that hands out the page, so one port carries both, and the client can connect back to its own host. Both ideas are from the WebSockets readings.
@"client !== socket": The one change from the chat room. There, the sender got its own message back and displayed it like everyone else's. Here that echo would be a disaster: a client would receive its own offer and answer itself.
@"data.toString()": What gets forwarded is the raw text. The server never parses it, never checks whether it is an offer or a candidate, and would keep working if the message format changed completely.

step: The rest of the file
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/server.js
region: static-server
The rest is the [static file server](<../Module 2 - Server-Side JavaScript/03 - Serving Static Files.webnb>) from Module 2, Lesson 1, serving the page and its script out of `static/`. Nothing about WebRTC appears anywhere in this file.
```

The signaling server does not know what an offer is, and does not need to.

## The Client, Before Any Call

`static/main.js` runs in both windows. It starts with the two servers' addresses and one button:

```{walkthrough}
title: Setup
step: The page
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/index.html
@"connect-button": The button starts disabled. Pressing Connect before the signaling channel exists could not work, so the page does not offer it, the same way the last reading's editor stayed disabled until the document arrived.

step: Two servers, one object
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/main.js
region: setup
@"new WebSocket(": The signaling channel: a WebSocket to whatever host served the page, exactly as in the chat room, and over `wss:` when the page itself arrived over `https:`. If the signaling server ran as a separate program on its own port, you would spell out that address here instead.
@"stun:stun.l.google.com": The configuration names the STUN servers to use, and this is Google's public one. The peer connection contacts it on its own; no more code is needed for the STUN side.
@"let peerConnection = null": Nothing is created yet. Each side builds its peer connection when a call actually starts, and which line does that depends on which side of the call you turn out to be.

step: Enabling the button
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/main.js
region: enable
@"removeAttribute": The button becomes clickable only once the WebSocket to the signaling server is open.
```

## Making an Offer

Clicking Connect makes your window the caller:

```{walkthrough}
title: The caller's side
step: The click handler
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/main.js
region: offer
@"setAttribute('disabled'": Disabled again immediately, so a double click cannot send two offers.
@"createOffer": Produces the session description: a `type` of `'offer'` plus a description of what this side can send. It returns a promise, hence the `await`.
@"setLocalDescription(offer)": Create it, remember it as your own side's description, send it. All three, in that order.
@"JSON.stringify({ type: 'offer'": The offer travels as text through the signaling server, wrapped in a labeled message like the one we printed earlier.

step: Creating the connection
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/main.js
region: create-peer-connection
@"new RTCPeerConnection(configuration)": One end of the connection, pointed at the STUN server by the configuration.
@"addEventListener('icecandidate'": As the browser discovers routes to itself, this event fires once per candidate. Each one is forwarded through the signaling server so the other side can try it.
@"if (iceEvent.candidate)": The event also fires once with no candidate to say "that was all of them", which is what the check filters out.
```

In the video, these listeners are written in the property form, `peerConnection.onicecandidate = ...`, and the WebSocket ones as `ws.onmessage = ...`. They are the same events with the same arguments; `addEventListener` is the form we have used all course.

## Handling What Arrives

Everything the other side sends comes out of the signaling server as a message, and one listener sorts them out:

```{walkthrough}
title: The dispatch
step: Three kinds of message
file: p2p-demo/01-connection/static/main.js
region: handle-messages
@"message.type === 'offer'": Somebody wants to connect to us. Their offer describes their side, so it becomes our **remote** description.
@"if (!peerConnection)": The answering window never clicked Connect, so its peer connection does not exist yet. It gets created here instead, by the same function the caller ran in its click handler.
@"createAnswer": The reply. An answer describes *our* side, so it is saved as our **local** description and sent back through the signaling server.
@"message.type === 'answer'": The offer we sent worked. Their answer describes their side: remote description again, and nothing to send back.
@"addIceCandidate": A route by which the other side might be reached. Every arriving candidate is handed to the peer connection, which tries the routes and settles on one that works.
```

Three kinds of message, three responses:

- **an offer**: set it as the remote description, create an answer, save the answer locally, send it back.
- **an answer**: set it as the remote description. Done; you made the offer.
- **a candidate**: add it to the peer connection.

Notice that "set the other side's description as my remote description" happens in both of the first two branches. It is the same step seen from either end, and it is how both sides end up holding both descriptions.

## The Dispatch, Running

The dispatch is the part to be sure of, so here it is running against stand-ins:

```{javascript autorun}
const peerConnection = { local: null, remote: null, candidates: [] };

peerConnection.setLocalDescription = function (description) {
  peerConnection.local = description.type;
};

peerConnection.setRemoteDescription = function (description) {
  peerConnection.remote = description.type;
};

peerConnection.createAnswer = function () {
  return { type: 'answer' };
};

peerConnection.addIceCandidate = function (candidate) {
  peerConnection.candidates.push(candidate);
};

const sent = [];

function handleSignal(message) {
  if (message.type === 'offer') {
    peerConnection.setRemoteDescription(message.offer);

    const answer = peerConnection.createAnswer();

    peerConnection.setLocalDescription(answer);

    sent.push('answer');
  } else if (message.type === 'answer') {
    peerConnection.setRemoteDescription(message.answer);
  } else if (message.type === 'candidate') {
    peerConnection.addIceCandidate(message.candidate);
  }
}

// Somebody else reaches out to us first.
handleSignal({ type: 'offer', offer: { type: 'offer' } });
handleSignal({ type: 'candidate', candidate: 'route-1' });
handleSignal({ type: 'candidate', candidate: 'route-2' });

console.log('remote description: ' + peerConnection.remote);
console.log('local description:  ' + peerConnection.local);
console.log('candidates known:   ' + peerConnection.candidates);
console.log('sent back:          ' + sent);
```

An offer came in, an answer went out, and two routes were recorded. Both descriptions are set, which is what the connection needs.

## Running It Yourself

A handshake between two windows is not something one notebook cell can show you. Run it:

```
$ cd "p2p-demo"
$ node 01-connection/server.js
Listening on http://localhost:3000
```

Open `http://localhost:3000` in **two** browser windows, open the console in both (right-click, Inspect, Console), and click **Connect** in one of them. Then:

1. The window you clicked logs `Sending an offer to the signaling server`, and the *other* window logs that an offer arrived and an answer went back. Whichever window you click is the caller; the other answers without anyone touching it.
2. In the Network tab, find the request the page made to `ws://localhost:3000` and look at its Messages: the offer and the answer are right there as text. That text is everything the signaling server ever sees.
3. Do not be surprised if few or no ICE candidates appear yet. Nothing is attached to this connection, so the browsers have nothing to route, and they may not bother finding routes for it. The next reading attaches a camera and a microphone, and the candidate traffic appears.

The connection exists, and it is carrying nothing. That is the next reading.

## Exercises

1. Complete the signaling server's forwarding so a message reaches everyone except its sender.

    ```{node}
    const OPEN = 1;

    function makeClient(name, readyState) {
      const client = { name: name, readyState: readyState, received: [] };

      client.send = function (data) {
        client.received.push(data);
      };

      return client;
    }

    const alice = makeClient('alice', OPEN);
    const bob = makeClient('bob', OPEN);
    const carol = makeClient('carol', 3);

    const clients = [alice, bob, carol];

    function forward(fromSocket, data) {
      // TODO: send data to every open client except the one it came from
    }

    forward(alice, 'an offer from alice');

    console.log(alice.received, bob.received, carol.received);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const OPEN = 1;

    function makeClient(name, readyState) {
      const client = { name: name, readyState: readyState, received: [] };

      client.send = function (data) {
        client.received.push(data);
      };

      return client;
    }

    const alice = makeClient('alice', OPEN);
    const bob = makeClient('bob', OPEN);
    const carol = makeClient('carol', 3);

    const clients = [alice, bob, carol];

    function forward(fromSocket, data) {
      for (const client of clients) {
        if (client !== fromSocket && client.readyState === OPEN) {
          client.send(data);
        }
      }
    }

    forward(alice, 'an offer from alice');

    console.log(alice.received, bob.received, carol.received);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(bob.received).should('equal', ['an offer from alice']).run('Every other open client should receive the message', 'Forwarded to the other client');
    wrap(alice.received).should('equal', []).run('The sender must not receive its own message back, or it would end up answering its own offer. Compare each client with the sending socket', 'Skipped the sender');
    wrap(carol.received).should('equal', []).run('A client that is not open should be skipped, as in the chat room', 'Skipped the closed client');
    ```

